p6l5-teachhand.note:
Minutes 58.5 to 61.5. Teach before worksheet page 2 (Q4, and the Q6 code as the bottom panel; Q5 routes is the take-home question, so there is no live teaching for it). Think-aloud: “5 friends shaking hands. Friend A shakes 4 hands. B has already shaken A’s, so 3 new ones. C: 2 new. D: 1 new. E: none left. 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 10. Or: 5 people times 4 hands is 20, but each handshake has two people in it, so I counted every one twice: 20 ÷ 2 = 10.” Ask: "With 6 friends?" (5 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 15, or 6 × 5 ÷ 2). Do not do 7 friends: that is the worksheet. Code panel: the loops check every pair (a, b): 5 × 5 = 25 pairs; a = b is not a handshake; a < b keeps 1 and 2 but not 2 and 1, so each handshake once. Run it live if you can: it says 10. Ask: "What would it say with a > b?" (10 again). Python version:
count = 0
for a in range(1, 6):
 for b in range(1, 6):
 if a < b:
 count = count + 1
print(count, "handshakes")
